                <pb facs="0051" n="51" xml:id="pg51"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Seward to Mr.
                            Judd</hi>.</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 16.]</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><hi rendition="#small-caps">Department of
                            State</hi>,<lb/><placeName><hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Washington</hi>,</placeName>
                        <date calendar="gregorian" when="1861-10-21"><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >October</hi> 21, 1861</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Your despatch of September 24 (No. 8) has
                    been received. It treats of many matters interesting, though not of chief
                    importance. Your proceedings in regard to them are approved. Disunion, by
                    surprise and impetuous passion, took the first successes, and profited by them
                    to make public opinion in Europe. Union comes forward more slowly, but with
                    greater and more enduring vigor. This nation, like every other, in the present
                    as in all other cases, stands by its own strength. Other powers will respect it
                    so long as it exhibits its ability to defend and save itself. More, perhaps,
                    ought not to be wished; certainly it could not be reasonably expected.</p>
                <p>I am, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>WILLIAM H. SEWARD.</signed></closer>
                <postscript>
                    <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">N. B Judd</hi>, Esq.,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                            >&amp;c., &amp;c., &amp;c., Berlin</hi>.</p>
                </postscript>
            </div>
